Should prisoners be part of the franchise-or-suffrage?:-

1. Why are prisoners currently denied the vote? - This is because they are regarded as having renounced the rights of citizenship for the duration of the time that they are incarcerated.
2. Who is John Hirst and how has he tried to change this? - Hirst was in prison but claimed that he should be allowed to vote as it was a fundamental human right, this was dismissed by the courts in 2001.
3. What role did the European Court of Human Rights play here, and how did this put the British government in an awkward position? - In 2004 the European Court of Human Rights declared that the blanket ban on prisoner voting was contrary to Article 3 of the First protocol of the European Convention on Human Rights, which ‘provides for the right to elections performed by secret ballot, that are also free and that occur at regular intervals.
4. Name two pressure groups who have campaigned in this area. - Liberty and the Howard League for Penal Reform
5. What is the view of the public and parliament? Be specific. - Very little public pressure for it unlike voting at 16. 234 MPs against and 22 for.
6. What action did the Government propose in 2017 and why? - they offered a small number of prisoners on day release (category D) the option to vote, they did this because it had been causing friction with the EU
7. What is your view? Should certain groups of prisoners be allowed to vote? - Absolutely yes. Many young people are groomed into crime then disenfranchised. Rehabilitation is proven to work better than punishment and reintegration into society is an important part of this.

Should 16 year olds be able to vote:-

- Political parties would have to listen to young people more 
- The belief that young people are apathetic is wrong
- 16 year olds already have many responsibilities
- Adults are not necessarily more political aware or educated 
